# VEIL patch series, applied in order on top of the pinned tor-browser tag.
#
# Empty until the first patches are cut against the confirmed 16.0 tag on a
# build machine (they must be generated with `git format-patch` from real
# commits so they apply cleanly). Planned first patches, in order:
#
#   0001  [branding] VEIL name, icons and update URL in browser/branding/veil
#   0002  [chrome]   Register about:veil-home from veil-chrome, with its parent
#                    actor answering the start page's bridge messages and
#                    handling Sign In via src/VeilSignIn.sys.mjs and the
#                    app side panel via src/VeilAppPanel.sys.mjs
#   0003  [network]  Install src/VeilAnonymityGuard.sys.mjs at startup: VEIL
#                    goes offline if any setting would let traffic bypass Tor
#   0004  [network]  Remove every UI path that turns Tor off or adds a direct
#                    connection (one always-anonymous workspace, no switching)
#   0005  [update]   Point the updater at VEIL channels and VEIL MAR keys
#
# None of these may change what web content can observe; veil-qa's
# fingerprint diff against stock Tor Browser gates every build on that.
